    "Q10.1": "substantially",
    "Q10.2": "more_supportive",
    "Q10.3": [
      "specific_tradeoff",
      "intervention_design_matters"
    ],
    "Q10.4": "balanced_arguments",
    "Q3.4": "never",
    "Q10.6": "My primary realization during the workshop was that **model design must prioritize context over rigid thresholds**: Absalon data’s ‘never’ feels absolute because passive tracking risks framing delays as individual failure—while systemic factors (mental health, care responsibilities) require *support*, not surveillance. The group debate on Q3.4 demonstrated how *anonymized* usage could actually benefit equity if tied to opt-ins/outs *and* clear interventions. For disability-related data (Q3.5), I now see how it’s critical even if predictive accuracy is uncertain—because proactive support saves careers/degrees (like mine nearly lost). However, the workshop also showed that without a **shared governance** framework, no model will earn student trust.",
    "Q6.1.other_text": "",
    "Q10.3.other_text": ""
  }
